# Docker deployment environment (production Compose).
# Create .env.production and fill the required secrets:
#   node scripts/init-production-env.mjs   # creates .env.production + fills secrets
# Then run the stack explicitly against it:
#   docker compose --env-file .env.production -f docker-compose.yml up -d
# (Passing --env-file replaces the default .env as Compose's interpolation
# file — the dev .env is never read for deployment, though host shell
# environment variables can still override individual values. Conversely, no
# dev tooling (pnpm dev / vite / drizzle / vitest) ever reads this file.)
# Source-build verification (contributors / PR acceptance) builds THIS
# checkout explicitly and runs the same file against the local tag (#626):
#   docker build --target runner -t exam-local:dev .
#   EXAM_IMAGE=exam-local:dev \
#     docker compose --env-file .env.production -f docker-compose.yml up -d

# ── Required secrets (no defaults; init-production-env fills, never rotates)
JWT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Topology ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_USER=exam
POSTGRES_DB=exam

# Prebuilt production images, pinned to the release version. init-production-env
# derives both from .release-version (ghcr.io/jnhu76/exam and
# ghcr.io/jnhu76/exam-web, vX.Y.Z); an explicit value always wins. Offline
# installs: pull on a connected machine, `docker save`, transfer,
# `docker load`, keep these same references.
EXAM_IMAGE=
EXAM_WEB_IMAGE=

# Public HTTP ingress port published by the nginx edge (container stays 80;
# API 3000 / Web 4173 are internal service ports — nginx is the only public
# production ingress, #585). Anything other than the default 80 (or any
# non-localhost address) must set CORS_ORIGIN / PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN to the
# address users actually browse.
# EXAM_PORT=80
# CORS_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.5
# PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN=http://192.168.1.5

# APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Shanghai
# TZ=Asia/Shanghai

# ── Optional: trusted reverse proxy (bundled nginx edge, #585) ───────────
# CIDRs whose sockets may rewrite the client IP via X-Forwarded-For
# (comma-separated; a bare IP means that one host). Unset = trust nobody:
# request.ip stays the socket address and forwarding headers are ignored.
# PRECONDITION: cover ONLY the proxy→API link — NEVER the candidate client
# network (and never a client LAN CIDR): a trusted CIDR that covers clients
# lets them forge their rate-limit/audit identity. Trust-all CIDRs
# (0.0.0.0/0, ::/0) are rejected at startup. The bundled edge
# (deploy/nginx/edge.conf) is directly client-facing and REPLACES
# X-Forwarded-For with the real client address ($remote_addr — appending
# would let clients forge the header inside the trusted walk). When
# candidates are served through it, trust exactly the stack's own bridge
# subnet — look it up after the first `up`:
#   docker network inspect <project>_exam-net   # e.g. 172.19.0.0/16
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=172.19.0.0/16
# Direct-LAN deployments without the edge keep this unset — see the
# deployment runbook §2 "Rate-limit identity, trusted proxies, and sizing".

# ── Optional: Redis (enable with `--profile redis`) ──────────────────────
# REDIS_PASSWORD=<secret>
# REDIS_URL=redis://:<secret>@redis:6379

# ── Optional: email (defaults: disabled, fake transport — nothing sent) ──
# EMAIL_ENABLED=true
# EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.your-org.internal
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=

# ── Optional: first-install launchpad token ──────────────────────────────
# LAUNCHPAD_SETUP_TOKEN=
